  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 2 letters from Genoa (1616) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: East Indies (1), China (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 1,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "January 3, 1616",
    "place": "Genoa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 284",
    "excerpt": "The fire that burns within my heart does not permit, Reverend Father in Christ, that I, as a son to so loving a father, should not lay bare the good desires that burn continually within my breast. I wrote, while the happy memory of our Father Claudio Acquaviva was still alive, to that Father, asking leave to go to the Indies; and having received from him more than a loving reply, for the love and affection which, without any merit of mine, he showed me, bidding me make myself ready and keeping remembrance of this my desire, I could no longer defer making Your Paternity aware of it — Your Paternity alone being the one who, after God, can fulfill this my desire.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The fire that burns within my heart does not permit, Reverend Father in Christ, that I, as a son to so loving a father, should not lay bare the good desires that burn continually within my breast. I wrote, while the happy memory of our Father Claudio Acquaviva was still alive, to that Father, asking leave to go to the Indies; and having received from him more than a loving reply, for the love and affection which, without any merit of mine, he showed me, bidding me make myself ready and keeping remembrance of this my desire, I could no longer defer making Your Paternity aware of it — Your Paternity alone being the one who, after God, can fulfill this my desire. I ask it with the greatest affection that I can, per misericordias Domini (through the mercies of the Lord), able to say no more, and with all humility of heart; and I assure Your Paternity, Coram Domino (before the Lord), that no greater consolation in this life could I receive than this — to go to those blessed and happy parts of the East Indies, if it so please Your Paternity, to toil, to suffer, and to die in the most obscure place that may be found, for love of my sweetest Jesus and Mary, and for the salvation of those souls. All things I leave, however, in the hands of Your Paternity, so that if it seem to you — whether because of my short time, which does not exceed eighteen years and six months, or for my studies, now learning Rhetoric, having studied part of last year, or for whatsoever other reason — to prolong the time, the will of my Lord Jesus and of Your Paternity be done; although, as for me, I should esteem myself most happy to be able to leave this Italy, already odious to me for my desire for those parts. And not to be more tedious to Your Paternity, I shall make an end, assuring you that my soul will not be at rest until I have some good hope that at least, after I have finished the course of Philosophy — if to this I shall be admitted by my superiors — I might obtain this my desire; and with a holy envy of those who have already been notified in this province, with heart and with the greatest affection I can, I put myself in the hands of Your Paternity, commending myself to your holy prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 2,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "January 5, 1616",
    "place": "Genoa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 285",
    "excerpt": "The fire that burns within my heart, Reverend Father in Christ, does not permit me, as a son, to withhold from Your Paternity the good desires that burn within my breast. I wrote, while the happy memory of our Father Claudio Acquaviva was still alive, to that Father, asking leave to go to the Indies; and having received from him — for the love and affection he showed me, not for any merit of mine — a most courteous reply, telling me to provide myself with the arms necessary for such a voyage, inasmuch as he held particular remembrance of this my desire, I could no longer delay resorting to Your Paternity, who alone, after God and the most blessed Virgin Mary, can fulfill this my desire; which I ask with all the humility and the greatest affection of heart that I can.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The fire that burns within my heart, Reverend Father in Christ, does not permit me, as a son, to withhold from Your Paternity the good desires that burn within my breast. I wrote, while the happy memory of our Father Claudio Acquaviva was still alive, to that Father, asking leave to go to the Indies; and having received from him — for the love and affection he showed me, not for any merit of mine — a most courteous reply, telling me to provide myself with the arms necessary for such a voyage, inasmuch as he held particular remembrance of this my desire, I could no longer delay resorting to Your Paternity, who alone, after God and the most blessed Virgin Mary, can fulfill this my desire; which I ask with all the humility and the greatest affection of heart that I can. And I assure Your Paternity that no greater consolation can I have in this life than this — that the grace be granted me to go to labor, to toil, and to die in the most obscure place that may be found in the world, for love of my Jesus and the salvation of those souls. All things, however, I remit to the hands of Your Paternity, so that if you be pleased that I wait yet some time — I pray Your Paternity, if the delay must be, that it be as brief as possible — the will of the Lord be done, whether to study the course of Philosophy, if to this I be admitted by my superiors, whose will I desire perfectly to fulfill. I put myself wholly in the hands of Your Paternity; dispose of me as shall most please you, for I should esteem myself most happy to be able to depart once from this Italy with these Father Procurators — of China or of Japan — this Italy which I hold almost in hatred for the desire I feel for those blessed and happy parts. And with this, making an end, I pray Your Paternity, per misericordias Domini (through the mercies of the Lord) — I can say no more — to keep some remembrance of this your son, and to commend him to the Lord in your holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-3",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Lelio Antoglietta",
  "author_raw": "Lelio Antoglietta",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "china",
  "dossier": "Wrote 1 letter from Naples (1616) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: China (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 3,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "January 8, 1616",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 291",
    "excerpt": "Know, Your Paternity, that for no short time the talent of the Second Vocation has burned within my breast, which God has given to me, most unworthy of such a talent. I have considered, indeed, that keeping it hidden, without revealing it to Your Paternity, is a most clear sign that I am not beginning to take the road of putting it into execution; and because I do not wish to fall into the sin of ingratitude toward the Giver of such a talent, I say plainly to Your Paternity that for about a year (not recalling the exact time), and perhaps more, in my cold prayers, examinations, and communions, and likewise through the day, I feel myself impelled and called to pour out the life which I have given to Jesus (by means of vows of devotion) in the lands of the infidels, among Chinese, Indians, Japanese, and such others as are found in the world.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Know, Your Paternity, that for no short time the talent of the Second Vocation has burned within my breast, which God has given to me, most unworthy of such a talent. I have considered, indeed, that keeping it hidden, without revealing it to Your Paternity, is a most clear sign that I am not beginning to take the road of putting it into execution; and because I do not wish to fall into the sin of ingratitude toward the Giver of such a talent, I say plainly to Your Paternity that for about a year (not recalling the exact time), and perhaps more, in my cold prayers, examinations, and communions, and likewise through the day, I feel myself impelled and called to pour out the life which I have given to Jesus (by means of vows of devotion) in the lands of the infidels, among Chinese, Indians, Japanese, and such others as are found in the world. The reasons that have moved me to make this request are not few; but lest I be too long, I shall tell some of them. Let the first be what I have said above — that I feel myself impelled and called, etc., and therefore I wish to correspond. Let the second be this: that I see clearly that I am by nature most choleric, and I know clearly (from the experience I have had in the world) that woe to me if I employ it for evil; but likewise I know that if I employ it for good (with God's grace) I hope to accomplish something in the service of God. But because Your Paternity might say to me that I have already employed it for good by entering religion, and that therefore there is no fitting reason for the confirmation of what I seek to obtain, to this I answer, saying that I am of a certain nature — that if I should suffer some grave displeasure in religion (granting, though, that I took it to heart), it could easily happen, while remaining in these parts, that I should commit some fault by which I could endanger the holy state of religion; but on the other hand, if what I seek in this letter were granted to me, then, besides that I would not have in those parts the ready occasion that I have in these, etc. — but I know full well that such apprehension could have no place in my mind, partly because I am by nature constant in such matters, partly also because the continual occupations which are had in the welfare of souls, all these things, would rescue me from such apprehension and danger. The third, my Father — to speak plainly — is that I am most proud by nature, and therefore it is the easiest thing in the world that all the labors which I should spend for God in religion, were it in studies, would be employed not for God but for honor (and therefore adding, I say to Your Paternity with all my heart that it would please me much more to go to such countries as a Brother Coadjutor than as a scholastic, if there be easier and readier opportunity of going); and to remove such occasion of sin, and to perform such labors to the greater glory of God, I seek to go to such parts, there being neither convenience nor occasion to fall into such diabolical snares of ambition and honor. The fourth reason is because Your Paternity must consider that I have not offended God in the way that some coeval of mine (for his age) has offended Him; but know, my Father (a thing to be wept by me, wretch, with tears of blood), that I have offended Him more than any man of my age in the world could and knew how to offend; and it is an incredible thing to explain with paper or pen. And for my greater confusion, God was not content with having called me to religion the first time, and likewise the second, I like metal or hard diamond in corresponding; but even the third time, and beyond all these calls, the warnings and admonitions which God spoke through the mouths of my teachers, confessors, and fathers of congregations; and a thing indeed marvelous to tell, even through the mouth of the very Mother I had in the world; and now likewise called by so (by me unmerited) a vocation. So that, my Father, to correspond to some particle of the so many benefits God has done to me, I demand, ask, and pray for the above-said. The fifth reason is: that I may detach myself from everything which by chance I could desire; and especially that I may no longer have occasion to converse with the relatives I had in the world: cutting all this off by going to these lands so desired by me, so that with ease I may obtain and keep what I have promised to God.",
     "The sixth reason is: that I may have ready occasion to suffer for love of Him who, for love and the good of my soul, suffered so much: such countries being apt to satisfy my desire. The seventh reason is: for having learned in the annual letters that, for lack of workers, so many souls are lost, for whose salvation Jesus willed to be nailed to the cross. The eighth reason is to be able to say to Jesus at my last breath with truth that it was not for want of me that I did not shed my blood for love of Him: and how could I say this if I did not go to these countries, requested by me (with all the affection of my heart)? I finish, my Father, by telling you that up till now I have said nothing of what my heart would fain vent. I return to repeat the above-made supplications, demanding this for the paternal love which he bears to the Society, his most beloved daughter; seeking this voyage for the filial affection and love which Your Paternity has rooted in his ardent and inflamed heart toward the Mother of Jesus (together God), Mary the Virgin, our mother; finally praying for the obligation which Your Paternity has toward His Divine Majesty for the great desire (which like fire burns the heart of Your Paternity) to love, serve, and honor Him — as (without deceiving myself at all) I am certain that Your Paternity thinks, speaks, and works of nothing else: and all this the most just and fitting reasons, which, not to offend the humility of Your Paternity, I leave unsaid, move me to say. Concluding, I finish by asking your pardon for my lengthiness; but let Your Paternity believe me truly that my heart has not yet given satisfaction in saying what I would say; but if I would say it, neither with words nor in writing could I explain it — for what else would I say than that I cannot explain it? I wish to say, and cannot? I desire and wish to express it, and cannot? And as your most unworthy son I ask at last your holy blessing; and, if so you wish and it be convenient to you, let Your Paternity tell me in your reply the things that would be an impediment (in my person, though) to the voyage so desired by me, so that I (with holy obedience and indifference) may answer and oppose them, with God's grace."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-4",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Ignazio Balami",
  "author_raw": "Ignazio Balami",
  "age": 24,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 3 letters from Messina (1616) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: East Indies (3).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 4,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "January 14, 1616",
    "place": "Messina",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 311",
    "excerpt": "I write the present to Your Paternity, which I had proposed to write to you long ago, but on account of the multitude of my sins and for the ingratitude of not having corresponded to a promise made to God — made once my general confession on entering religion was finished, gotten through cheaply at the time — but planted now with most bitter tears and not without grave scruple. The reasons I had not done it before were first my sins, then because I saw the door shut and, so to speak, I was ashamed too, because the matter would have dragged on long and would have been known to all; for which, my most loving Father, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the bowels of Jesus Christ), I pray Your Paternity to pardon me and so to act, for your paternal charity, that I may be one of those who expose their lives in the uttermost parts of the world for the Holy Faith, that I may in part satisfy God for my sins and for the promise made to Him, as I have touched upon above.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write the present to Your Paternity, which I had proposed to write to you long ago, but on account of the multitude of my sins and for the ingratitude of not having corresponded to a promise made to God — made once my general confession on entering religion was finished, gotten through cheaply at the time — but planted now with most bitter tears and not without grave scruple. The reasons I had not done it before were first my sins, then because I saw the door shut and, so to speak, I was ashamed too, because the matter would have dragged on long and would have been known to all; for which, my most loving Father, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the bowels of Jesus Christ), I pray Your Paternity to pardon me and so to act, for your paternal charity, that I may be one of those who expose their lives in the uttermost parts of the world for the Holy Faith, that I may in part satisfy God for my sins and for the promise made to Him, as I have touched upon above. I have now received communion, and at once I took up the pen and wrote to Your Paternity what the Lord inspired in me. Let not Your Paternity fail to console me, for the ardor is so great that I desire nothing else, think of nothing else, long for nothing else but this journey, by means of which, through the mercy of the Lord, I hope for the salvation of my soul, as of some neighbor. For the full information of Your Paternity: seven years ago I entered the holy religion, and I have already studied philosophy, and I am now teaching in the 2nd class of the College of Messina, and by the divine grace I am healthy enough to serve for everything. I hope to make myself holy by this road. I am of the age of twenty-four years. We are three brothers in the Society. In sum, my most loving Father, let Your Paternity know that my heart desires nothing else but this journey; and I promise Your Paternity that in all my labors and in all my actions I shall remember to pray the Lord — though unworthily — to grant Your Paternity such grace that you may govern in the same way as our blessed Reverend Father Ignatius would govern and did govern, he whose name I bear for the devotion which my parents had to him.",
     "I finish writing, but not speaking with my heart, both to God and to Your Paternity, so that if they shall judge this to be both for the service of God and for the profit of my soul and of my neighbors, it may be put into execution as soon as possible — that, while my strength aids me, as I offended Him with so many sins, so I may satisfy Him by shedding my blood for His service and the help of the souls redeemed with His most precious blood. This is what my Christ communicated to me in holy communion. Your Paternity, as one guided by the Holy Spirit, will judge it; and know that so great is the confidence I have in God and in His most glorious mother that I have already put myself in order as if I were to depart tomorrow. I finish by asking Your Paternity's blessing, praying Heaven for every fullness of happiness, commending myself to you with all affection."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 11,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "February 27, 1616",
    "place": "Messina",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 380",
    "excerpt": "I write the present to Your Paternity with tears in my eyes, so great is the pain I feel at having made known so late this great desire of mine to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world and to shed my blood for love of Him who, for me, a most ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross. I thank Your Paternity for the greeting and for the good hope you give me, for I hope that one day I shall be content. Let Your Paternity know that several times a day I withdraw to weep and to pray the Lord that He forget my ingratitude and deign to hear me, for truly I see and know that this is the road by which I may begin to hope for my salvation and in some measure make satisfaction to God for the multitude of my sins; for the mere thought of it now makes me strive to attend with all diligence to spiritual things and to find difficulty in nothing, and what in the past I shunned, I now desire and pursue with affection and consolation — for which may the Lord be blessed forever.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write the present to Your Paternity with tears in my eyes, so great is the pain I feel at having made known so late this great desire of mine to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world and to shed my blood for love of Him who, for me, a most ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross. I thank Your Paternity for the greeting and for the good hope you give me, for I hope that one day I shall be content. Let Your Paternity know that several times a day I withdraw to weep and to pray the Lord that He forget my ingratitude and deign to hear me, for truly I see and know that this is the road by which I may begin to hope for my salvation and in some measure make satisfaction to God for the multitude of my sins; for the mere thought of it now makes me strive to attend with all diligence to spiritual things and to find difficulty in nothing, and what in the past I shunned, I now desire and pursue with affection and consolation — for which may the Lord be blessed forever. Therefore, my most beloved Father, let Your Paternity not fail to console this soul, afflicted with good reason, by deeming me worthy of admission into the number of those holy men who go to die for the Holy Faith and for the name of Jesus and Mary; and do not look upon my nothingness, for I hope that the Lord, who calls me, will give me grace to serve Him in some thing. I do not feel that I have yet given vent to my heart; but lest I weary you further, I end by humbly asking Your Paternity's blessing, praying you to hear a nobody such as I. Let Your Paternity know that in all my labors I shall never forget to pray the Lord, unworthily to be sure, that He grant you those graces which are needful for a Father General of the Company of Jesus, so that Your Paternity may be another Blessed Father Ignatius."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 19,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "June 2, 1616",
    "place": "Messina",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 462",
    "excerpt": "The ardor and the desire to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world, and where I may suffer most for love of Him who for me, an ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross, goes on growing so much that it does not permit me to linger long in consoling myself by unburdening it in some measure with Your Paternity. Know then that my desires, my thoughts, my consolations, and when I can, even my words, are in nothing else than this — holding myself, however, ever unworthy before the eyes of the Divine Majesty.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The ardor and the desire to employ myself in the farthest parts of the world, and where I may suffer most for love of Him who for me, an ungrateful sinner, died upon the rough wood of the Cross, goes on growing so much that it does not permit me to linger long in consoling myself by unburdening it in some measure with Your Paternity. Know then that my desires, my thoughts, my consolations, and when I can, even my words, are in nothing else than this — holding myself, however, ever unworthy before the eyes of the Divine Majesty. Let Your Paternity forgive me if I trouble you, for I have no other refuge. I pray you, with what affection I know and can, to make me worthy of admission into the number of these saints who expose their lives for the Holy Faith; and let Your Paternity know that this befits me, that by this means I may begin to make satisfaction to that God whom I have so offended with the multitude of my sins and wickednesses. I have great hope of obtaining this, for I assure myself that Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, will know this need; and nothing else moves me but this, having great scruple at not having begun to ask since I promised it to the Lord when the General Confession was finished in the novitiate. I end without ever ending, anew in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ), praying you to console me and not to look upon my nothingness, for He who calls me will see to it that I may be a good subject for this ministry; and I shall never forget to pray the Lord that He grant you so much spirit and grace that you may be another Blessed Father Ignatius. To the Holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself with all affection."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
