  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 22,
    "year": 1616,
    "date": "December 9, 1616",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 506",
    "excerpt": "I believe it belongs to sons, in all their needs, to have recourse to their Father, so that according to their needs they may be provided for. If in temporal things it is so, with much reason — and more still — it is so in spiritual things, to the spiritual Father; and it seems to me that in this there occurs another thing as well, namely that not only our needs, but all other things too pertaining to the service and the greater glory of His Divine Majesty, must be made known to those Fathers — among which things are vocations.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I believe it belongs to sons, in all their needs, to have recourse to their Father, so that according to their needs they may be provided for. If in temporal things it is so, with much reason — and more still — it is so in spiritual things, to the spiritual Father; and it seems to me that in this there occurs another thing as well, namely that not only our needs, but all other things too pertaining to the service and the greater glory of His Divine Majesty, must be made known to those Fathers — among which things are vocations. Having done this up to now, there occurs to me at present another thing, which belongs only to Our Father: the vocation to the Indies, a very great grace of God, and to be most greatly esteemed. It goes in this manner. Two years before I was admitted to the novitiate, or a little less, hearing talk of the things of the Indies — particularly of the martyrdoms — since from childhood I have always had the vocation to the Company, I felt certain interior movements, and was much taken with these things. Afterwards those movements kept growing within me, though I did not recognize the grace of God; these movements grew much with the occasion of certain missions which ours were conducting through Naples, with whom I used to go along to help at least to sing through the streets, to draw the people. When I felt those movements, since I did not recognize that grace, I said: if in small things I cannot master myself, how will I carry myself there? Entering the novitiate, and being in the Exercises, my desire grew greatly, and I began to know it was truly a thing of God. And so I offered myself to Him, and thanked Him. This caused me the consideration of the preciousness of a soul. One evening, if I am not mistaken, making the reflection of the prayer, my heart was kindled with this desire; and I did as usual, namely to say: if in small things I do not master myself, what will I do in greater ones? And having said this, I felt an answer within me: Potens est Deus de lapidibus (God is powerful, even from stones) — but interiorly. And this I have communicated to various superiors I have had, by whose counsel I have not written to Your Paternity before; but now, with the counsel of my [Father], I have written. For the [...] let us leave aside the other things concerning the same vocation, which I hope to write to you another time. All this I commend to God, saying, quid me vis facere (What wilt Thou have me do?), and to Your Paternity, His vicar. To Your Paternity I make a thousand reverences, and commend myself to you with all my heart."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 28,
    "year": 1617,
    "date": "March 23, 1617",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 124",
    "excerpt": "Because blessed God does not fail, with His infinite mercy, as He has deigned to give me this holy desire to go to the Indies, so also to keep it in me, indeed to increase it ever more, giving me great hope of one day enjoying its fulfillment: so I believe it is not amiss to recall it to Your Paternity from time to time, accompanying it with the prayers of others and of mine; for by this means I hope to obtain this grace from His Divine Majesty, whom I have ever found so liberal toward me that I have good reason to promise myself as much, and more, for the future — although on my part I see no fitness at all, either for this or for anything else.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Because blessed God does not fail, with His infinite mercy, as He has deigned to give me this holy desire to go to the Indies, so also to keep it in me, indeed to increase it ever more, giving me great hope of one day enjoying its fulfillment: so I believe it is not amiss to recall it to Your Paternity from time to time, accompanying it with the prayers of others and of mine; for by this means I hope to obtain this grace from His Divine Majesty, whom I have ever found so liberal toward me that I have good reason to promise myself as much, and more, for the future — although on my part I see no fitness at all, either for this or for anything else. And yet this very thing gives me great hope and confidence; for infima mundi eligit Deus, ut confundat fortia (God has chosen the meanest things of the world, to confound the strong). I pray Your Paternity, therefore, for the love of God and of the Blessed Virgin, to deign to remember me — though may the greater glory of God and of the Most Blessed Virgin always be done, to whom I commend this business and this letter. I make a thousand humble reverences to Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 47,
    "year": 1618,
    "date": "July 25, 1618",
    "place": "Nola",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 63",
    "excerpt": "Although I am most certain, as indeed I have known, that Your Paternity, by your charity, keeps fresh memory of me — for which it would not be so necessary to annoy you with my letters — nevertheless I do not seem fully to satisfy myself unless I seek, by letters, not being able otherwise, the execution of what I supremely desire in this life. All the more so as that first ardent desire has not at all lessened; indeed I believe I may say it, and with truth certainly, that now it burns more and urges me on.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although I am most certain, as indeed I have known, that Your Paternity, by your charity, keeps fresh memory of me — for which it would not be so necessary to annoy you with my letters — nevertheless I do not seem fully to satisfy myself unless I seek, by letters, not being able otherwise, the execution of what I supremely desire in this life. All the more so as that first ardent desire has not at all lessened; indeed I believe I may say it, and with truth certainly, that now it burns more and urges me on.",
     "Nor, by His infinite goodness, does blessed God fail continually to kindle it with the wind of His Mercy, and to dispose me with a certain hope — of bearing cheerfully both the torrid zones and the perils of the ocean, the furies of heaven, the tempests of the sea, the journeys by land, hunger, thirst, perils, labors, and whatever inconvenience might befall the body. Believe me, my Father, and if I am long, forgive me, that the mere memory consoles me, and hope recreates me; for: Ille terrarum mihi praeter omnes angulus ridet (that corner of the world smiles on me above all others) — “That corner of the earth smiles on me beyond all others” — as said the Venusian Poet. Honey, yes, truly honey — of celestial consolations, which yield neither to nectar and ambrosia, nor to all the delights of all time, nor even to the spiritual tastes which the persons of our Society continually experience in their chambers in these parts — of which our Father Xavier gives most clear testimony.",
     "It is a sweet thing to think, to speak, and even to write of these things, particularly to him from whom, after God and the aid of the saints, I await the execution, as I hope, and have some pledge, or earnest, of it; but I would not abuse the gentleness, courtesy, and charity of Your Paternity; therefore I close, humbly making you reverence, desirous of being able to pray for you from atop a cross. Amen."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 51,
    "year": 1619,
    "date": "January 3, 1619",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 736, 2",
    "excerpt": "Considering this heroic action which the Son of God performed eight days after His Nativity, my heart grows tender, and for two reasons which move me to supplicate Your Paternity and to seek from you a most rich offering — what we call an inferta — I also feel driven to render to this most sweet and most loving Child some sort of return. One reason is that, if He, so early, begins to suffer and shed His blood for my sins, I who am the guilty one ought not to remain idle, not only to seek to mortify myself but also to give, or at least to expose, my life for love of Him, according to my vocation.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Considering this heroic action which the Son of God performed eight days after His Nativity, my heart grows tender, and for two reasons which move me to supplicate Your Paternity and to seek from you a most rich offering — what we call an inferta — I also feel driven to render to this most sweet and most loving Child some sort of return. One reason is that, if He, so early, begins to suffer and shed His blood for my sins, I who am the guilty one ought not to remain idle, not only to seek to mortify myself but also to give, or at least to expose, my life for love of Him, according to my vocation. The other reason is that the Child takes the name of Jesus, and sheds His blood, while I most unworthily am to call myself a member of the Company of Jesus, without shedding my blood for so precious a name given to me by Jesus Himself out of His infinite goodness — certainly not; but with divine grace I shall ever strive to answer my vocation, the outcome of which I humbly and suppliantly seek from Your Paternity. I add to all this that, since so many called to this enterprise have so fully answered it, it does not seem fitting to me, nor do I satisfy my vocation, unless I burn with desire to put it into execution; I hope to do so exactly, and shall ever strive more and more to prepare myself for the enterprise with the favor of the Blessed Virgin, to whom long ago I commended this matter, which I know most evidently to have come from God, for His greater glory and my perfection. So I finish, and, as I said, this offering I seek from Your Paternity by the blood of the Child Jesus, and by the love He shows us; and I assure Your Paternity that, if I obtain this prize, I shall be rich, and nothing else shall be lacking to me, and there shall be nothing left for me to desire but that — trusting in the goodness of God — I may yet hope to pray for Your Paternity above a cross, or amid irons, or in some other sort of torments; but meanwhile I promise not to fail to provide myself with the grace of God, and may Your Paternity remember this poor wretch. So I pray to God to give you a most happy new year, a blessed Easter, and every height of spiritual happiness; and together with Baldassarro [—] I commend myself to Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 54,
    "year": 1620,
    "date": "May 29, 1620",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 736, 156",
    "excerpt": "My hopes — strengthened by the encouragement I have on many occasions received from Your Paternity — of sailing, for the love of Christ who calls me to it, to the farthest parts of the world, make me think that, if my sins do not oppose it, on this present occasion they might be fulfilled. May they be fulfilled through the mercies of Jesus, and by the abundant blood He lavished for me; and by such liberality may it come about that I too show myself liberal with my own. [...] This sole consolation I desire, and I well know that long waiting ought to count as nothing in order to acquire what I desire, [...] ...",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "My hopes — strengthened by the encouragement I have on many occasions received from Your Paternity — of sailing, for the love of Christ who calls me to it, to the farthest parts of the world, make me think that, if my sins do not oppose it, on this present occasion they might be fulfilled. May they be fulfilled through the mercies of Jesus, and by the abundant blood He lavished for me; and by such liberality may it come about that I too show myself liberal with my own. [...] This sole consolation I desire, and I well know that long waiting ought to count as nothing in order to acquire what I desire, [...] ... Commending myself to the paternal charity of the Most Blessed [Mary], I remain with sounder and more living hopes, and I make Your Paternity most humble reverence."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 56,
    "year": 1621,
    "date": "September 30, 1621",
    "place": "Salerno",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 736, 288",
    "excerpt": "Although, as I have always done, so also now I place myself in the hands of Your Paternity concerning going to the East Indies or else to the West, nevertheless — as I have also sometimes signified to Your Paternity — I have the greater desire, in the hope there is of shedding blood for love of Jesus, to go to the Eastern Indies; whence, having heard some time ago that Father Laerzio would soon be in Rome — [thinking] that he is already there — I have wished anew, with the resignation [...], to renew before Your Paternity my desires, reminding you that, my philosophy being finished, with the time of the voyage and a little more besides, I could, once sent there, employ myself in the salvation of those peoples.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although, as I have always done, so also now I place myself in the hands of Your Paternity concerning going to the East Indies or else to the West, nevertheless — as I have also sometimes signified to Your Paternity — I have the greater desire, in the hope there is of shedding blood for love of Jesus, to go to the Eastern Indies; whence, having heard some time ago that Father Laerzio would soon be in Rome — [thinking] that he is already there — I have wished anew, with the resignation [...], to renew before Your Paternity my desires, reminding you that, my philosophy being finished, with the time of the voyage and a little more besides, I could, once sent there, employ myself in the salvation of those peoples. I stand at the door, and knock. Your Paternity well knows I am in good bodily health, thanks be to the Lord, who will doubtless supply my other failings. So, everything placed in the hands of Your Paternity, I make you humble reverence."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 60,
    "year": 1624,
    "date": "December 5, 1624",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 737, 222",
    "excerpt": "At the news that has come here of the good harvest prepared in Ethiopia, where two hundred of ours are called, my ancient desire has been kindled further, and I have entered into hope that into that numerous [company] there may [enter] one most lowly and unworthy of the name and title of soldier of Christ. My Father, remember me for love of God, and do not look at my little being, but at the infinite power of Him who called me and keeps me in this holy desire, and who delights and glories in raising up the needy from the earth [inopem (a poor one)].",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "At the news that has come here of the good harvest prepared in Ethiopia, where two hundred of ours are called, my ancient desire has been kindled further, and I have entered into hope that into that numerous [company] there may [enter] one most lowly and unworthy of the name and title of soldier of Christ. My Father, remember me for love of God, and do not look at my little being, but at the infinite power of Him who called me and keeps me in this holy desire, and who delights and glories in raising up the needy from the earth [inopem (a poor one)]. The desire is matured, unless I err; the call is from God; the execution rests with God, entrusted — as the least interpreter, guided by the same God — to Your Paternity, to whom I make humble reverence, praying you to give me the Holy blessing."
    ]
