    "archival": "ARSI, FG 735, 276",
    "excerpt": "Very Reverend Father, Our Father,\n\nThe great longing which I have always had, and which I now feel within me more than ever, to go to the Indies has moved me to write these four lines to Your Paternity — especially now that the opportunity is favorable to go to Japan, toward which I aspire with all affection. I was ever desirous, even from a young age, of spending my life for the salvation of souls, and of shedding even my blood for Him who, in shedding His for them, was so lavish of it. So when I looked upon the image of the martyrs, I felt myself at once grow tender and burn with love for such a lot; and I said within myself: O, if God, who granted these so great a grace, would grant to me as well, I could do everything!",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Very Reverend Father, Our Father,",
     "The great longing which I have always had, and which I now feel within me more than ever, to go to the Indies has moved me to write these four lines to Your Paternity — especially now that the opportunity is favorable to go to Japan, toward which I aspire with all affection. I was ever desirous, even from a young age, of spending my life for the salvation of souls, and of shedding even my blood for Him who, in shedding His for them, was so lavish of it. So when I looked upon the image of the martyrs, I felt myself at once grow tender and burn with love for such a lot; and I said within myself: O, if God, who granted these so great a grace, would grant to me as well, I could do everything! And then there came to mind all the comforts, [pleasures], and paternal means, and I said, as I say anew now: away with kindred, honors, pomp — if only I too may at last be made worthy to suffer for my Jesus. This was the principal cause which inspired in me the vocation to the Company, and for this same reason God our Lord preserves me in it; for I never feel so consoled as with the consolation that fills me when an occasion offers to do something in aid of souls. And every time I have asked this (which I have often done, especially to Father Ottaviano Navarola while I was a novice, and to the Father Provincial while in the College), I have felt within me so great a consolation that, overflowing outwardly, I seemed to melt with spiritual joy; and though I did not obtain what I asked, it did not pain me — rather it helped me to grow more in spirit, so that, if it were God's will, I might obtain it one day. In prayer I never felt so much consolation as I felt then; then I found myself readier for obedience and for the exercises of the Holy Religion; and, to say all in one word, it always seemed to me I had made more profit in the spirit whenever I had disclosed such a desire to my Superior. I have always known, moreover, that this was communicated to me by the Lord God through the gifts which from that same God I have received, fitting me for such a vocation — namely, such health as I could not seem to desire greater; for I was never ill in the Religion except eight days of measles. Scarcity of food and fasting do me no harm, but rather good — fasting with the grace of the Lord through Lent; and finally from bodily and mental labors I have never felt harm to my health. I will end my writing, for it seems to me utte